#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063840 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/id #uuid "fe39d0a2-20cd-5d77-8801-c1a8575706e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10876" :question/problem "Calculate $F(\\pi)$ where $\\displaystyle F(x) = \\int_1^x \\dfrac 1 t\\,\\textrm d t$." :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\pi$, we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\!\\left(\\pi\\right) = \\int_{1}^{\\pi} \\dfrac{1}{t} \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(π) &= ∫_{1}^{π}\\frac{1}{t}dt \\\\\n&= \\ln \\mid t \\mid \\mid _{1}^{π} \\\\\n&= \\ln π - \\ln 1 \\\\\n&= \\ln π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063841 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "426cd4bd-909d-527f-a35c-9bb10ef3e734" :question/math-academy-id "e-10877" :question/problem "Suppose that a pool has a small hole and that water is leaking out at a rate of $t^{1/3}$ gallons per minute, where $t$ is the time in minutes. If at time $t = 0$ the pool contains $100$ gallons of water, how many gallons of water will remain in the pool after $t$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(t) = -t^{1/3}\n$$\n\nbe the rate at which the volume of water in the pool is changing, and let $F(T)$ be the amount of water in the pool after $T$ minutes. Note that $f(t)$ is negative to indicate that the volume is decreasing.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nF(T) = F(0) + \\int_0^T f(t)\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(T) &= F(0) + ∫_{0}^{T}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= 100 - ∫_{0}^{T}t^{1/3}dt \\\\\n&= 100 - \\frac{3t^{4/3}}{4} \\mid _{0}^{T} \\\\\n&= 100 - (3(T)^{4/3})/(4) + (3(0)^{4/3})/(4) \\\\\n&= 100 - \\frac{3}{4}T^{4/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t$. We have\n\n$$\nF(t) = 100 - \\dfrac {3}{4}t^{4/3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063842 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "834e0019-f098-5b33-8579-2fae3f46ba78" :question/math-academy-id "e-10884" :question/problem "The graph of a function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Given $\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{-2}^{t} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x$, determine the value of $g(3)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/333/Source/Images/6a389b5bb31f7ee2ea2b8194d93fdc6d.png)" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3$, we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/333/Source/Images/0c342ebdf2da41b0cc85cdb1342abbcd.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x \\in [-2,0]$, for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_1$.\n- $x \\in [0,3]$, for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is another trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_2$.\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{-2}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x \\in [-2,0]$, we compute the area $A_1$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n$A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot (2 + 3); = 5$\n- To find the second integral where $x \\in [0,3]$, we compute the area $A_2$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot (2 + 4); = 9$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(3) &= ∫_{-2}^{3}f(x)dx = A_{1} + A_{2} = 5 + 9 = 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6e447f3-327c-5000-bc0c-2f468f1b4916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061259} {:answer/id #uuid "cb40830c-dba2-5d0e-bef7-3acad933cf54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061260} {:answer/id #uuid "e67dd1ab-fd32-5d15-8b28-fe0183f10b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061261} {:answer/id #uuid "f5826fb7-6e44-5d8e-831b-ac6c510880bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061262} {:answer/id #uuid "0b3bd5ac-32f6-582f-9d3d-ab124c90c8f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5826fb7-6e44-5d8e-831b-ac6c510880bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061262} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a56bac-fbb7-5704-9c39-8cfb62bd543a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061258}] :question/id #uuid "abd361ea-0dd1-53a8-8717-46e052a5b559" :question/math-academy-id "q-155514" :question/problem "Calculate $F(3)$ where $F(x) = ∫_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{t}dt$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answe